#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e03093-45e3-5a29-8e56-5c627469229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324158} {:answer/id #uuid "96f5d810-b983-5dd0-9374-08ba9c895889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325257} {:answer/id #uuid "c22e9da9-c19b-5939-ae6a-6ea72bf20ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325638} {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4f72-3e21-5735-891d-271c0479b06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325968} {:answer/id #uuid "f4622c11-3b33-54f5-ad6f-ba2120f1d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4f72-3e21-5735-891d-271c0479b06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325968} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9007cd6-14d2-52e3-b682-7062e520d6ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04ff9ec0-bc04-5bc6-a60f-5b7459d9c0ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40662" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${A}^{T}=A$\n- ${A}^{2}=A$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true since both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8e0b89-42bf-519d-9d97-5be58994dc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327337} {:answer/id #uuid "43e90ae2-41ae-5a8b-bd0d-a84be897dc3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327526} {:answer/id #uuid "cd965883-d1dd-572c-b5e0-9d1d81b810b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328742} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d7cf84-3b4e-527a-beaf-e866d3ca0aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328938} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f0e7-80e6-54c9-b9ec-b1cb786a0558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e0b89-42bf-519d-9d97-5be58994dc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327337} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a52cef-1ba2-5963-a084-9155ceff6344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef67ff18-5c70-5c00-bdf0-27b79dc90b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-43306" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $A$ has two identical rows\n\nII. One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n\nIII. $A$ has two identical columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10778a8b-7dba-5224-9e37-47a0aadec5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327082} {:answer/id #uuid "a4311801-5138-5fda-a1d0-047b32a3b223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328355} {:answer/id #uuid "d59c2d87-1ef9-57e1-b2cb-7cb68d03fce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328802} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c08adc-5f73-5580-84a6-853eac64f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328836} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ae25fd-16ab-5fef-bc5f-e15edc6b79c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ae25fd-16ab-5fef-bc5f-e15edc6b79c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329086} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8fef60-6273-5a72-b265-d3cad6fa4786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65ce77e2-71c5-5054-a9e0-2b1a859bf906" :question/math-academy-id "q-43981" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4767179b-e760-5407-b987-281c5a994c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327557} {:answer/id #uuid "6b926fe7-010d-5765-9b05-57838d45ac48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327860} {:answer/id #uuid "86a36528-0f78-50c5-9a03-d78ef2331796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328094} {:answer/id #uuid "bd47911c-9b82-5690-9142-16046e5e2086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328584} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbbd8bb-9a46-54da-a559-203f0e5113e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86a36528-0f78-50c5-9a03-d78ef2331796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328094} :answer-field/id #uuid "25309370-e18c-5f8d-b9d3-a52855a34253" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d8b1c2-9248-513d-b061-309a07f2a6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\n- ${Q}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The columns of the identity matrix are orthonormal. Therefore, $I$ is an orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Computing the product $2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41142936-cfbe-5358-9b91-3e2d43f3c58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358304} {:answer/id #uuid "70a72cff-725b-53d4-b2cf-8f7f18a9bb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358735} {:answer/id #uuid "bc46cd74-5213-519d-8f9e-edaff37ef6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359388} {:answer/id #uuid "db810e3f-d7a4-5a4f-ab4b-03f9496d6199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359675} {:answer/id #uuid "f7701a76-35a6-5241-b465-7ea12079f8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db810e3f-d7a4-5a4f-ab4b-03f9496d6199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359675} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b84e28-d1a0-561a-90f6-59485ff13324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f8ab8c-12d7-5df5-b151-ee8250c362bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96833" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6180fa-2359-5a99-b2b5-34128319c8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358412} {:answer/id #uuid "7092b4b7-325b-5d7b-b9c6-c4bd41b138b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358733} {:answer/id #uuid "83d09de0-992e-5b1e-a4ea-45adb87c5408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358911} {:answer/id #uuid "bc73eac1-a2fb-5cac-8557-c079683d8c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359390} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8e6bf0-b059-5de6-9ae3-ca00875b1dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7092b4b7-325b-5d7b-b9c6-c4bd41b138b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358733} :answer-field/id #uuid "42d61567-5eab-57c4-ad4f-14de1b67d5e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f527ee5b-22e1-5696-9bd4-0e444190bb44" :question/math-academy-id "q-97305" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,0⟩=0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $2⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-2b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the antilinearity in the second argument we have that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,0⟩ & =⟨a,0\\cdot 0⟩=\\overset{―}{0}⟨a,0⟩=0⟨a,0⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,-2b⟩=\\overset{―}{(-2)}⟨a,b⟩=-2⟨a,b⟩\\ne 2⟨a,b⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number, in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f9020e-83ec-5df7-80bf-cc00b4552045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357913} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3a03df-0544-5fe2-8d9e-660b17164ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358262} {:answer/id #uuid "4760fcbe-f25b-58f0-b9b7-40841152fb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358366} {:answer/id #uuid "6f977601-6585-5dd5-9942-3f690514f370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358722} {:answer/id #uuid "7082d1bc-5f39-5a4b-8cb1-aab831d836b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4760fcbe-f25b-58f0-b9b7-40841152fb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358366} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2385db-7d71-5467-9779-556ead63ba51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c169d7e-31f7-5721-948b-24c63fd291d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97451" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fa0f9c-bae5-51cb-8799-a9c8917d8f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333870} {:answer/id #uuid "9abe6754-b348-56ad-9faa-a28fca8c1fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad826703-d06c-542f-b160-56dcd681b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334520} {:answer/id #uuid "ae32c2a5-78fd-5cd9-a0b3-e8646064834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334524} {:answer/id #uuid "cea03520-191b-5259-a7a2-7ad74d03b7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae32c2a5-78fd-5cd9-a0b3-e8646064834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334524} :answer-field/id #uuid "76582c08-f455-5c5c-a722-a27fb8767982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55cfa82a-9072-54fc-bfe1-353f6a508e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-64576" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={10}^{2}-4(1)(25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =13>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398637b9-9de8-5603-8107-020a5725e120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333526} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6f2b94-98ce-59eb-9977-cd986817c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333844} {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334052} {:answer/id #uuid "d124aebd-c863-5507-aa4c-186618654ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334815} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a5947-95ad-5807-ba8c-d3f854bb8495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4eec8b-2a2a-5ef1-a4bf-644458b1a312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a95c4f-6f0b-5fe1-b32c-ae9f75fde633" :question/math-academy-id "q-64617" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277ea4c2-b095-5302-8554-096a68fb2d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336400} {:answer/id #uuid "71dc7733-2769-570f-8ea5-f271a16e4888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337051} {:answer/id #uuid "8c31f83e-254b-5fa6-acd0-6920afa48391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337302} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d7e380-ed7f-5aa8-bddb-a9dbeeaf4491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337516} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6d5a8c-a78a-5649-b2ca-f8c09e74fcaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c31f83e-254b-5fa6-acd0-6920afa48391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bace304-8adf-5d3c-bdab-afe547432273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddee53f3-5b0d-5dc0-9b9e-825db292f3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71110" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842e95d-7478-5e61-8e97-0569f4aba80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336700} {:answer/id #uuid "5e795725-b836-5864-ab56-5d08aa98f334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336890} {:answer/id #uuid "674e190c-095c-5a32-a660-8e0e15b8da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336955} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9ba1ff-d028-59eb-91d3-ed4a2a264754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337329} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5d65cf-8f26-5d2d-b7f3-57567712e43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842e95d-7478-5e61-8e97-0569f4aba80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336700} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b8455e-1c0e-5ab5-a911-7a1e4db64e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8314953-42cd-5ac5-902d-92a3c570bac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71112" :question/problem "Given the two planes $-x-2y+z=0$ and $2x+4y-2z+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y-2z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z+1=0 \\\\ x+2y-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ -x-2y+z-\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cd2439-d216-5cbf-bf00-7ad9407dacb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241491} {:answer/id #uuid "2b44f6e5-88b2-590f-96b6-ef4b64192cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241562} {:answer/id #uuid "811d0610-0dd3-509c-a04f-c67e87559e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242311} {:answer/id #uuid "b49460a7-1387-524f-bfec-7e510bcd5344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242758} {:answer/id #uuid "bd073272-d100-5bc2-8647-20da76fb7a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23cd2439-d216-5cbf-bf00-7ad9407dacb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241491} :answer-field/id #uuid "3750e017-5e8e-5458-a060-9ecd41a3fa30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4b429ab-49a9-5137-bfd6-13315591e96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109243" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-5I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$\n\nII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n\nIII. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149569} {:answer/id #uuid "30eef23f-1a55-5ac4-8a57-9c63c6f949e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149570} {:answer/id #uuid "5b128d60-e594-5aa5-bff5-a1e2fa17357d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149571} {:answer/id #uuid "66c8be3b-ec27-54c3-a77d-bf9d20dd0b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149572} {:answer/id #uuid "7410e289-bd9b-5799-8292-33605aba5faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149569} :answer-field/id #uuid "692a1b0c-9534-5975-bd7a-3a92a99093ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a51040-e179-5b66-9e0b-fca0e478f23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214171" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={0,1,2}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(0,2),(2,0)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because  $(0,0)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* transitive because $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\in R,$ but $(0,0)\\notin R$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6491d9-1d90-5386-995a-284889bdd49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409351} {:answer/id #uuid "2b860813-d4b1-5fef-b506-6ffcc8c555af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409357} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4173a3-d315-5fb7-9f00-3bd771b4750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409359} {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409387} {:answer/id #uuid "b68067c2-b8d7-5f62-b933-7366f89a68bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7ce273-1469-5ebe-853b-6ac76cc6e24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc725849-ec72-5565-a2a3-c18e7bade536" :question/math-academy-id "q-76784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409342} {:answer/id #uuid "05752e2f-49d8-5029-8640-38d4fb2c4302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409344} {:answer/id #uuid "79a9d6c6-5a61-5865-93e5-90b3c4aec498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409379} {:answer/id #uuid "d45d7b30-c602-5bbb-b327-69ce48a4fb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409408} {:answer/id #uuid "e8143651-dbed-5f90-b38d-d9e1c91175c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409342} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27defb4-bb86-5b12-8d1d-2f258f68bd34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "665aa776-ec33-596f-ae58-1e8a076473e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76952" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28a50a6-7308-5e7d-9506-1e4175511186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151776} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8b1e51-4f39-5465-8987-f4a307226cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151777} {:answer/id #uuid "171962fe-cdfc-56f5-897b-aa03946cf77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151778} {:answer/id #uuid "61310617-8554-57e3-9ce9-7002b33a7a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151779} {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151780} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4be8de-607c-5578-91b6-081cf9216fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-2$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-2$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""}]}
